Commit:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Thu Oct 1 23:07:10 2026 -0500

    fix: fail closed on unparseable rule files (PyYAML-less fresh host). Addresses TJ-DF-040.
    
    Posture change: on a host without PyYAML the stdlib-JSON fallback is the
    ONLY path a .yaml rule file can take, so the JSON-fallback parse failure
    covers EVERY installed rule file — the shipped 00-builtins.yaml mirror
    included. Until now _load_file swallowed that failure and returned [],
    so the whole firewall silently degraded to allow-everything while the
    sandbox layer still ran; the loud stderr note from TJ-DF-039 named the
    file but the bridge verdict stayed a plain allow.
    
    Now the fallback failure raises RuleFallbackParseError (new, subclass of
    RuleParseError) after its one loud note; _load_file refuses it and the
    load ABORTS, so the existing TJ-GAP-070 engine-exception path turns it
    into the blocking [bridge-error] verdict. Fail open survives only for
    two NARROW, named arms (no more blanket `except Exception`):
    
    - RuleParseError (real PyYAML parser rejected the SYNTAX): DF-6's
      documented skip-with-warning leniency, narrowed to the class it was
      written for — only reachable when PyYAML is importable, so it cannot
      mask the fresh-host shape.
    - OSError (unreadable file): one bad permission must not brick the whole
      ruleset; the skip is announced via the new UNREADABLE note.
    
    With PyYAML present behavior is byte-identical (control tests/probes).
    
    Also updated: the two-stage-leniency docstrings in rules.py, the
    _rule_ids_in_file provenance note in decider.py, specs/interruptor.md
    (§3.5 failure-path split + §14 error handling) and docs/quickstart.md
    (engine-failure paragraph). Tests: new DF-040 class in
    test_bridge_fail_closed.py (bridge fail-closed verdict, loader abort,
    installed-mirror intercept abort, PyYAML control, named fail-open arms);
    the TJ-DF-039-era pins that asserted the old fail-open posture were
    re-pointed at the new posture (test_mirror_fails_closed_loudly...,
    builtin-blocks-via-engine-constants) instead of deleted.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
